I'm trying to create a multilingual full-text search function for my Netflix database that searches across English, Spanish, French, German, and Portuguese. I created a custom function that combines tsvectors from multiple languages and then built GIN indexes on the title columns using this function.

Here's my SQL:

```sql
CREATE FUNCTION to_tsvector_multilang(text) RETURNS tsvector AS $$
SELECT to_tsvector('english', $1) || 
       to_tsvector('spanish', $1) || 
       to_tsvector('french', $1) || 
       to_tsvector('german', $1) || 
       to_tsvector('portuguese', $1)
$$ LANGUAGE SQL STABLE;
CREATE INDEX movie_title_idx ON movie USING gin(to_tsvector_multilang(title));
CREATE INDEX movie_original_title_idx ON movie USING gin(to_tsvector_multilang(original_title));
CREATE INDEX tv_show_title_idx ON tv_show USING gin(to_tsvector_multilang(title));
CREATE INDEX tv_show_original_title_idx ON tv_show USING gin(to_tsvector_multilang(original_title));
CREATE INDEX season_title_idx ON season USING gin(to_tsvector_multilang(title));
CREATE INDEX season_original_title_idx ON season USING gin(to_tsvector_multilang(original_title));
```

The indexes are created successfully without any errors, but when I run queries like `SELECT * FROM movie WHERE to_tsvector_multilang(title) @@ to_tsquery('english', 'action')`, the query planner is doing sequential scans instead of using my GIN indexes. I've run ANALYZE on all the tables and the indexes show up in `\di`, but EXPLAIN shows they're not being used at all. The queries are extremely slow on large tables because PostgreSQL isn't leveraging the indexes I created.

What am I missing here? Why won't PostgreSQL use these functional GIN indexes in my queries?

