I'm trying to clean up some data in my PostgreSQL lego database by replacing certain characters and normalizing whitespace in the `lego_parts` and `lego_sets` tables. I have three UPDATE statements that use `regexp_replace` to fix these issues:

```sql
UPDATE lego_parts
SET name = regexp_replace(name, '–', '-', 'g');
UPDATE lego_sets
SET name = regexp_replace(name, '\s+', ' ', 'g');
UPDATE lego_parts pp
SET name = regexp_replace(pp.name, '�', 'D');
```

The queries run without errors, but they're taking forever to complete on my production database. I'm seeing massive write activity and the queries are locking the tables for way longer than I expected. The `lego_parts` table has about 2 million rows and `lego_sets` has around 500k rows, but only a small fraction of them actually contain the characters I'm trying to replace. When I check `pg_stat_user_tables`, I see that all rows in both tables are being updated, even though most of them don't need any changes. This is causing bloat and triggering autovacuum which is making things even slower. Is there a way to make these UPDATE statements more efficient so they only touch the rows that actually need to be modified?

sol_sql:
UPDATE lego_parts
SET name = regexp_replace(name, '–', '-', 'g')
WHERE name ~ '–';

UPDATE lego_sets
SET name = regexp_replace(name, '\s+', ' ', 'g')
WHERE name ~ '\s\s';

UPDATE lego_parts pp
SET name = regexp_replace(pp.name, '�', 'D')
WHERE pp.name LIKE '%�%';

